ding the  lowest income americans  i started thinking about block power  really i was 22 23 years old i was a  community organizer i was trained by the  same community organizing group that had  trained barack obama and i was walking  around brooklyn one freezing winter day  it must have been like 21 degrees  fahrenheit and i looked around and all  of the apartment buildings had their  windows open you could see the steam and  the heat like pouring out of the windows  because the giant apartment buildings  and complexes were being overheated and  it reminded me of when i was a kid we  had to have our windows open in our  apartment during the winter on very cold  days because we did not have heat in our  building so we had to turn on the oven  open up the oven door and then we had to  open up a window to release carbon  monoxide  in 2019 new york city passed the climate  mobilization act a commitment to right  their wrongs  nearly 70 percent of new york's carbon  emissions are from their buildings and  the city aims by 2030 to cut their  pollution by 40 percent building owners  who don't meet the reduction  requirements will be faced with fines a  penalty of  268 dollars per every assessed ton of  carbon over the limit  what we're doing is trying to modernize  energy efficiency in green buildings by  building it right from day one with  software with mobile computing cloud  computing statistical analysis machine  learning using the internet of things  and low cost sensors  block power purchases clean energy  equipment we hire green construction  firms and we project manage them to  install green energy equipment in every  building and every home when a building  owner first is interested in working  with block power we have them fill out a  10-minute quiz or survey about how the  energy equipment in their building  currently works our software system  collects the data from the form we start  to run an analysis of what kinds of  clean energy recommendations make sense  for that building the building owner  gets a report from us that tells them  what kinds of  mix of clean energy equipment makes  sense for their building while building  owners may save money in the long run  the upfront investment in energy  efficiency can be insurmountable for  some based on data from the department  of energy the national average energy  burden for low-income households is  three times higher than for other  households one of the main reasons is  that low-income homes are more  frequently burdened with outdated  equipment and other poor housing  conditions that can lead to higher than  average energy use and costs groundswell  a non-profit renewable energy advocacy  group noted in an analysis that the  bottom twenty percent of earners spent  almost ten percent of their income on  electricity that's more than seven times  the portion of income that the top  twenty percent pays most americans don't  have you know twenty thousand fifty  thousand a hundred thousand dollars  laying around to convert your building  to make it all electric but if we can  stretch those payments out over time and  make sure that the monthly payment is  lower than what you're currently paying  for oil or for gas then that's an  attractive option for saving money and  saving the planet this model is already  being implemented in new york maryland  colorado and other states with incentive  programs for solar panels whereby low  interest loans allow homeowners to make  monthly payments that are comparable and  often less than typical electric bills  i think to date we've raised around 100  million dollars we have been really  fortunate to have some world-class  investors salesforce is an investor  goldman sachs the american family life  insurance corporation but there's four  trillion dollars of projects to do i  mean there's four trillion dollars worth  of buildings to decarbonize across  america  i think the most important thing that  we're doing is recently we were selected  by the city of ithaca to decarbonize the  entire city meaning every building no  fossil fuels we have an eight-year  timeline by which we have to decarbonize  the entire city so it's the first city  in america in the first city in the  world to fully decarbonize  we know that we have eight or nine years  to make massive interventions in the way  that we operate and the way that our  economy works in order to avoid the  worst impacts of climate change and if  we can decarbonize all cities across  america then 30  of our greenhouse gas emissions will be  avoided and that means america will have  led the world  away from the worst consequences of  climate change and so we're feeling the  pressure but we're really excited to try  and step up to the challenge
 

